<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
<%- include("partials/head", { title: "About - Irish language map", mapPage: false }) %>
</head>

<body>
<%- include("partials/nav", { active: "about" }) %>

    <div class="page">
        <header class="masthead">
            <img class="masthead-spot" src="img/spot-speaker.svg" alt="">
            <h1>About</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                A hand-drawn history of who could speak Irish, county by county,
                in every census from 1851 to 2022.
            </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prose">
            <%# A personal note from the maker, in a card. %>
            <aside class="note-card">
                <span class="note-tag">A note from me</span>
                <p class="note-hello">Dia duit!</p>
                <p>
                    I&rsquo;m Alessio Coci, and I made this site. It started with
                    something I couldn&rsquo;t stop noticing: in Ireland, everything
                    is in two languages. Road signs, forms, announcements, all in
                    English and Irish. And yet I hardly ever hear anyone speak
                    Irish. So <strong>where did the Irish language go?</strong>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The census has asked people about Irish since 1851, so the
                    answer was already out there, county by county. It just needed
                    drawing.
                </p>
                <p>
                    What I found is that it didn&rsquo;t simply disappear. It moved:
                    from the Atlantic coast to the whole of the Republic, and from
                    the home to the classroom. Today about 4 in 10 people say they
                    can speak it, but fewer than 2 in 100 speak it daily outside
                    school. The signs are bilingual; everyday life mostly
                    isn&rsquo;t.
                </p>
                <p>
                    If you spot a mistake, or you have a story about Irish in your
                    own county, I&rsquo;d love to hear it.
                </p>
                <p class="note-sign">
                    Alessio &middot; <a href="https://acoci.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">acoci.dev</a>
                </p>
            </aside>

            <h2>What is here</h2>
            <ul class="hand-list">
                <li><strong>The story.</strong> Scroll from 1851 to today: where Irish was strong, how it fell, and how it spread out again.</li>
                <li><strong>Your county.</strong> Guess its 1851 figure, then see its whole history against the Republic&rsquo;s average.</li>
                <li><strong>Every census on one map.</strong> The colours keep the same meaning in every year, so a shade is always the same share of people.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Thank you</h2>
            <ul class="hand-list">
                <li>
                    <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/phelimbirch.bsky.social" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phelim Birch</a>,
                    who compiled the Northern Ireland figures for his degree project
                    and generously shared them.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The Central Statistics Office, for the Republic&rsquo;s figures
                    (<a href="https://data.cso.ie/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data.cso.ie</a>).
                </li>
                <li>
                    Ordnance Survey Ireland and Ordnance Survey of Northern Ireland,
                    for the county boundaries.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                How the figures were put together, and how far to trust them, is
                on the <a href="methodology.html">methodology page</a>.
            </p>
        </div>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>
    </div>

<script src="js/nav.js?v=<%= assetVersion %>"></script>

</body>

</html>
